      "math_academy_id": "q-155559",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Represent the solution of the inequality $5x-3<2$ on a number line.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $3$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-3 & <2 \\\\ 5x-3+3 & <2+3 \\\\ 5x+0 & <5 \\\\ 5x & <5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & <5 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & <\\frac{5}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{5}x}{\\cancel{5}} & <\\frac{5}{5} \\\\ x & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<1.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14248115/assets/c1f7c992d9df85434eaf96e5bd9af52f9b6228e42fede6e9d4001e6471c3c239.png)",
